Answer: A looping control construct is used when the same task has to be executed repeatedly. For example, if a statement has to print the same text five times, writing the print statement five times creates redundancy in the code. Instead, the repeated statement can be written once inside a loop.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

This executes the print statement five times without repeating the same statement in the source code. The important idea is: Identify the repeated task. Write the task only once. Place it inside an appropriate loop. Use the loop control to determine how many times it should execute.

Answer: Suppose the requirement is to print "String Stack" five times. Writing:

```java
System.out.println("String Stack");
System.out.println("String Stack");
System.out.println("String Stack");
System.out.println("String Stack");
System.out.println("String Stack");
```

produces the required output, but the same code is unnecessarily repeated. A better implementation is:

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

The loop removes the redundancy because the statement exists only once while the loop controls its repeated execution.

