Answer: Consider:

```java
for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
if (i != 2) {
break;
}
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
}
```

When the inner loop begins: i = 1 The condition: i != 2 is true. Therefore, break executes immediately. Control leaves the inner loop before reaching the statement after the if. If the control flow guarantees that a statement can never be reached, the compiler/IDE may identify it as dead or unreachable code. The important point is to determine whether there is any possible execution path that can reach that statement.

Answer: Consider a nested structure:

```java
for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
if (i == 2) {
// break or continue
}
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
}
```

If break is used:

```java
if (i == 2) {
break;
}
```

the inner loop terminates for the current value of j. The outer loop can continue. If continue is used:

```java
if (i == 2) {
continue;
}
```

only the current inner-loop iteration is skipped. The inner loop continues with the next value of i. Therefore:

```java
break    → terminate the current loop
continue → skip current iteration and continue the loop
```

Answer: The same logic used with for loops can be implemented using while and do-while loops. The important requirement is to preserve the same behavior while changing only the loop construct. For example, a for loop:

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
if (i == 2) {
continue;
}
System.out.println("String Stack");
}
```

can be converted conceptually into a while loop while preserving: initialization condition check increment

```java
continue behavior
```

The same exercise can then be performed using a do-while loop. The objective is to understand that break and continue are jump statements whose behavior must be understood together with the control flow of the particular loop.
